# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# setup: benchrun
# board: only
# The bench, run against a mounted board from the shell.
#
# TikuBench builds, flashes and talks to boards; none of that is done
# again here.  What the shell adds is the board -- already mounted and
# named -- and the hand-over of its port, since a run that reflashes a
# board cannot share it with the shell that has it open.  So the whole
# question this asks is: does the shell let go, does the run reach the
# board, and does the shell get its board back?
wait 60
click 980 30
wait 10
menu-pick Applications
wait 10
menu-pick Bench
wait 250
expect-title Bench
note the suites are the bench's own list, not a copy of it kept here;
note down to link-console, which a board answers on its own; the list
note holds only what a board HERE can answer, so the count is stable
click-window Bench
wait 8
key Down
wait 3
key Down
wait 3
key Down
wait 3
key Down
wait 3
key Down
wait 3
key Down
wait 3
key Down
wait 3
key Down
wait 3
wait 8
click 71 8
wait 8
note the board is offered BY NAME, which is what the shell knows and
note the bench would otherwise have to be told
expect-menu Show the parts of link-console
expect-menu Run on nrf54lm20b
menu-pick Run on nrf54lm20b
note the results window is made only when the bench has said something,
note so its being here at all is the run's own report arriving
wait 900
expect-title Results
note and everything the run SAID is kept, not only what it meant: a run
note that fails before it means anything is not a dead end
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_bench_logs/link-console-nrf54lm20b.log [PASS] armed
note and the board is the shell's again: let go for the run, taken back
note after it, and answering as it did before any of this
wait 500
expect-read /devices/nrf54lm20b/sys/device/name tiku
quit
